<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <!--
    Common Controls v6 comes from the dependency further down: without it
    comctl32 v5 is loaded and every button and progress bar is drawn in the
    pre-XP style. Nothing errors; the wizard just looks two decades old, on the
    first screen a user ever sees.

    DPI awareness is the declaration below, and it is SYSTEM-AWARE ON PURPOSE.
    That is a decision with a measurement behind it, not a stepping stone to
    PerMonitorV2 — read the rest of this before "upgrading" it.

    Resolved 2026-08-17, correcting what was written here on 2026-08-15. The
    earlier note recorded that the client area came back as exactly 620x460 —
    the declared logical size — under PerMonitorV2, under system-aware, and with
    no manifest at all, and read "three declarations, one number" as evidence
    that the window never scaled. The observation was real and the conclusion was
    wrong: the probe was itself DPI-unaware, and Windows virtualizes every
    coordinate such a process reads, so it was shown physical pixels divided by
    the scale factor. Re-measured from a per-monitor-aware probe
    (`scripts/Measure-NativeWindow.ps1`) on the same 240-dpi machine, all three
    declarations produce a client area of 1550x1150 PHYSICAL — which is 620x460
    logical at 240 dpi, exactly right. Nothing was ever mis-scaled.

    They agree because the declaration is nearly moot: `winsafe` calls the legacy
    `SetProcessDPIAware` itself, from `initial_gui_setup`, on the first
    `gui::dpi()` call — which happens while the window options are being built,
    before the window exists. Removing this element entirely still produced
    1550x1150. It stays because a load-time declaration is not contingent on when
    some library first happens to touch DPI, and because it is the thing a reader
    looks for.

    PerMonitorV2 is not reachable here, and the blocker is the FONT rather than
    the geometry. `winsafe` 0.0.27 creates one `HFONT` for the whole process
    (`static mut UI_HFONT`, from `SystemParametersInfo`, which reports the system
    DPI) and sets it on each control as that control is created. So under
    PerMonitorV2 the controls would relayout to a second monitor's DPI while the
    text stayed the size of the first one's. Measured on this machine: Segoe UI
    9pt is a 41 px cell at 240 dpi, and on a 96-dpi monitor the heading label
    would be 26 px tall and the buttons 28 px. Every label clipped. System-aware
    instead gets a bitmap stretch from Windows — blurry, but proportionally
    correct and never clipped, which is the better failure of the two.

    Half of what this used to say is no longer true, and the half that changed is
    not the half that matters. `src/typeface.rs` DOES set a control's font now,
    from 2026-08-26, because the 9pt those figures describe was too small to read
    and the owner said so; it is `WM_SETFONT` through `SendMessage`, which
    `winsafe` marks `unsafe`, under a crate-local `unsafe_code = "deny"` that
    replaced the inherited `forbid`. What that does not give is a font that
    changes when the DPI does. Both fonts are built once from
    `SPI_GETNONCLIENTMETRICS` at `WM_CREATE`, at whatever the system DPI is, and
    nothing here handles `WM_DPICHANGED`. So the clipping above is exactly as
    reachable as it was — with a third more type in it.

    What would have to change to revisit this: rebuild both fonts and re-apply
    them on `WM_DPICHANGED`, alongside a relayout of every control, which
    `winsafe` 0.0.27 offers no help with; or build the wizard from a dialog
    resource template, which PerMonitorV2 rescales automatically INCLUDING its
    font, at the cost of an `.rc` compile step. The second is a toolchain change,
    so it needs asking first. Neither is a fix for anything anyone has reported —
    this machine has one monitor.

    One build trap, since editing this file is how anyone would test the above:
    `cargo:rerun-if-changed` compares mtimes, so restoring a manifest from a copy
    that preserved its timestamp reports `Finished` in under a second and leaves
    the PREVIOUS manifest embedded in the binary. Touch the file, then confirm
    what actually shipped by searching the `.exe` for a declaration only one
    version contains — `PerMonitorV2` served here. Not for text from a comment:
    the linker strips these, so nothing written between these markers reaches
    the binary and a comment cannot be used to identify the embedded version.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true</dpiAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>

  <!--
    Explicitly asOwner. This binary installs into the current user's profile and
    writes only HKCU, exactly as the NSIS installer it replaces did
    (`installMode: currentUser`), so it must not ask for elevation. A setup
    program that prompts for administrator when it does not need it teaches users
    to approve prompts they should read.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity
        type="win32"
        name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
        version="6.0.0.0"
        processorArchitecture="*"
        publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
        language="*" />
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11. The product is Windows-only and targets 11. -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
